  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings rely on visible encampments documented via in-person observations, which may underrepresent less visible or hidden unsheltered populations.",
      "what": "This study mapped and analyzed 11,225 visible homeless encampments in the urban core of Salt Lake City, Utah, using weekly in-person observations over an 18-month period. Using geospatial mapping, ANOVA, and negative binomial regression, the analysis found that encampments were largely absent from the downtown central business district but concentrated in open spaces, parks, and areas near light rail stations. Encampment visibility dropped sharply after August 2021, though specific northern zones of the city maintained highly persistent concentrations.",
      "why": "The research demonstrates that local political and enforcement decisions are stronger drivers of where unsheltered populations camp and persist than environmental factors, seasonality, or official land use classifications."
    }
